# The resolved model for the provider entry point (`npm run generate-provider`).
#
# model/sdk.json IS committed, because CI regenerates it and gates on drift.
# provider.json has no such gate: generating it requires the sibling
# ../../seneca/solardemo-provider checkout, which CI does not have, so a
# committed copy would go stale silently. An ungated generated artefact is
# worse than no artefact.
.sdk/model/provider.json

# Claude Code per-developer settings.
#
# The convention is `.claude/settings.json` SHARED and committed, and
# `settings.local.json` machine-local. Both of this repo's files were tracked
# under the .local name, so one developer's permission allow-list was imposed
# on everyone while its name promised the opposite. The shared allow-lists are
# now settings.json (a deliberate project decision); the .local files stay out.
**/.claude/settings.local.json

# ts/dist is COMMITTED on purpose (SECURITY.md: the repo can be cloned and
# tested without a build step). The generic `dist` rule above — inherited from
# the stock Node template's Nuxt block — matches a directory called dist at
# ANY depth, so it matches ts/dist too, and those 147 files are tracked only
# because someone force-added them.
#
# That is not cosmetic. While the rule stands, a NEW compiled file is invisible
# to `git status` and to `git add -A`: verified by dropping a probe file into
# ts/dist and watching `git add -A` ignore it. A genuinely new emitted module
# would simply never be committed, and the drift gate could not see it either,
# because it also works from `git status`.
#
# Un-ignored only for ts/dist. `.sdk/dist` and any other dist stay ignored:
# they are local build output, not shipped.
!/ts/dist/
!/ts/dist/**
